 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [2D Texture-Based Volume Rendering with Z-Slice Packing](4269-2d-texture-based-volume-rendering-with-z-slice-packing.md)
-Renders a 3D scalar field by storing Z-slices in a tiled 2D texture grid, allowing 3D sampling via 2D texture lookups and trilinear interpolation. It differs from standard 3D texture sampling by mapping 3D coordinates to a 2D atlas, requiring explicit slice indexing and offset calculation in the shader. Choose it when leveraging 2D texture hardware for volume rendering is required.
+What it is: Renders a 3D scalar field by storing Z-slices in a tiled 2D texture grid, allowing 3D sampling via 2D texture lookups and trilinear interpolation. What sets it apart: Differs from standard 3D texture sampling by mapping 3D coordinates to a 2D atlas, requiring explicit slice indexing and offset calculation in the shader. When to choose: Choose it when leveraging 2D texture hardware for volume rendering is required. Link: [2D Texture-Based Volume Rendering with Z-Slice Packing](4269-2d-texture-based-volume-rendering-with-z-slice-packing.md)
 
 ### [Point Cloud Volumetric Rendering via 2D Atlas](4879-point-cloud-volumetric-rendering-via-2d-atlas.md)
-A real-time GPU-accelerated technique for rendering 3D volumetric data (specifically point clouds or scalar fields) by storing the volume in a 2D texture atlas and rendering it as a point cloud where each point represents a voxel/sample. The depth of each sample is encoded in the atlas layout to allow correct sorting and rendering order without traditional depth buffering for the volume itself. Achieves 60Hz on GTX1080 for a 4k atlas, while Intel 630 graphics render a 2k atlas at ~20fps.
+What it is: A real-time GPU-accelerated technique for rendering 3D volumetric data (specifically point clouds or scalar fields) by storing the volume in a 2D texture atlas and rendering it as a point cloud where each point represents a voxel/sample. What sets it apart: Differs from standard volume rendering by encoding the depth of each sample in the atlas layout to allow correct sorting and rendering order without traditional depth buffering for the volume itself. When to choose: Choose it for real-time point cloud or scalar field rendering on constrained hardware. Link: [Point Cloud Volumetric Rendering via 2D Atlas](4879-point-cloud-volumetric-rendering-via-2d-atlas.md)
 
